// Lesson 10.1b — 3-Button Jam: Rapid Prototype
// Day 2 of the 3-Button Jam: greybox the core 3-button mechanic immediately using placeholder
// rectangles/circles and reused patterns so you prove the game is fun before polishing art.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1b — 3-Button Jam: Rapid Prototype</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    A great pitch on paper can still feel boring or awkward the second your fingers touch the
    keys. Today is <strong>Rapid Prototyping</strong>: build a rough greybox version where all
    three buttons work inside a plain test scene. No custom sprite art, no title screen, no music —
    only proving that pressing your three buttons feels responsive and fun.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Why Greybox? Speed Over Pretty (~15 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        If you spend two days drawing pixel animations before wiring movement and jumping, any
        redesign hurts because you already sunk hours into art. Professional jam teams prototype
        with <strong>squares, circles, and basic solid colors</strong> for one reason: if an idea
        fails a prototype test today, throwing away three squares costs nothing.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>What Belongs in Today's Prototype</th><th>What Waits Until After Playtesting</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td>All 3 <code>Input.GetKeyDown()</code> / <code>Input.GetKey()</code> verbs working reliably</td>
            <td>Final sprites, animations, backgrounds, particle effects</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Your riskiest movement, physics, or timing interaction</td>
            <td>Title menu, credits screen, multiple themed levels</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Reused patterns (triggers, <code>Instantiate()</code>, cooldown timers, TextMeshPro debug score)</td>
            <td>Sound effects and screen shake polish</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor + Code: Wire All 3 Buttons &amp; Reuse Patterns (~45 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>Build the Riskiest Part First:</strong> write only the scripts needed for your main object and its 3 inputs (for example: <code>rb.AddForceY()</code>, rotation/thrust, lane snapping, or spawning via <code>Instantiate()</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Borrow What You Know:</strong> reuse every Semester 1 habit cleanly — <code>[SerializeField]</code> tuning fields in the Inspector, <code>cooldownTimer -= Time.deltaTime</code> rates, <code>CompareTag()</code> triggers, and <code>OnCollisionEnter2D()</code> resets.</li>
        <li><strong>Put One Target / Obstacle in the Room:</strong> give your 3 buttons something to dodge, catch, shoot, or climb over so you can feel whether timing and distances click.</li>
        <li><strong>Run the 5-Second Fun Check:</strong> press Play with ONLY your 3 keys for 60 seconds. Ask yourself brutally: <em>Is just moving and pressing these three buttons engaging even with ugly squares?</em> If not, tune <code>speed</code>/<code>gravityScale</code>/<code>jumpForce</code> right now — or pivot before investing another day!</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Do not build level 1 before fixing button feel.</strong> A jam game lives or dies on
        its core 3-second loop (press → react → physics/reward). Tune your movement curves and
        cooldowns in one flat sandbox BEFORE designing obstacles around them.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Prototyping Honesty</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>After building your greybox prototype today, which of your three buttons felt the crispest right away, and which one felt clunky, slow, or confusing?</li>
            <li>What exact thing from Units 1–8 did you reuse inside today's scripts (<code>cooldownTimer</code>, <code>CompareTag()</code>, <code>Instantiate()</code>, <code>SetParent()</code>, <code>wayPoints</code>), and what part had to be written fresh?</li>
            <li>What happened during your 60-second test that surprised you — did your original pitch need a number tweak, a rule simplification, or a backup idea?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Play a greybox Unity scene where all 3 input buttons trigger distinct visible actions</li>
        <li>Interact with at least one placeholder obstacle, target, or timer without throwing errors</li>
        <li>Confirm the 3-button controls obey the strict 3-input limit from 10.1a</li>
        <li>Commit and push your working prototype cleanly</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — prototyping discipline</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — <code>Input</code>, physics velocities, timers, and <code>Instantiate()</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1c.html">10.1c — 3-Button Jam: Core Loop &amp; Self-Playtest</a> — complete win/lose + escalation and stress-test it yourself.</p>
</div>`;
